module Stryker
  # Turns a project file into instrumented source: the original source with
  # every mutant in it behind a switch, as
  # [Mutant placement](https://github.com/setdef/stryker_for_ruby/blob/trunk/docs/contributors/design/mutant-placement.md)
  # specifies.
  #
  # With no mutant active, the instrumented source runs the original code.
  # With one of its mutants active, it runs that mutant's text instead. Each
  # switch reads `::Stryker::Active.id` to choose, and calls
  # `::Stryker::Active.hit` with the IDs of its mutants each time its original
  # branch runs. The instrumenter doesn't define `::Stryker::Active`: the
  # runtime of the process that loads the instrumented source does.
  #
  # The instrumenter splices each switch into the original bytes, around the
  # node it wraps, so everything outside the switches stays as it was:
  # comments, heredocs, magic comments, and the data after `__END__`. Every
  # line of the original stays on the line where it was, so `__LINE__` and
  # the line numbers in backtraces are unchanged.
  #
  # Some mutants get no switch. The result lists each of them with the
  # reason, so that a run can report each of them with a status and say why:
  #
  # - `:no_switch_site`: no node around the mutant's site can hold a switch:
  #   inside `BEGIN { }`, or at a class or module name, or at a statement of
  #   a kind that the placement table doesn't name, such as `@count = 0` or
  #   a `for` loop, at the top level of a file or directly in a class or
  #   module body.
  # - `:no_one_line_form`: the mutant's text spans several lines, and has no
  #   form on one line, as a regexp with a line break inside it, or its plain
  #   mutant parses but has no one node in the parentheses where its text
  #   goes, as when the text starts with a line break.
  # - `:does_not_parse`: the plain mutant, the original file with the
  #   source of the node that the switch wraps replaced by the mutant's text
  #   in parentheses, doesn't parse, as when `x = f` becomes `x = return`,
  #   whose plain mutant `x = (return)` is a void value. A run reports such a
  #   mutant with the status `CompileError`.
  #
  # The instrumenter loads Prism and refract. Load it only in the
  # orchestrator, never in a process that loads the project's code.
  #
  # ## Examples
  #
  # Two mutants in one expression, where the switch of the mutant at the
  # inner site sits in the original branch of the outer one:
  #
  # ```ruby
  # require "stryker/instrumenter"
  #
  # source = "total = a + b * c\n"
  # mutants = [
  #   Stryker::Mutant.new(id: 7, file: "lib/invoice.rb", mutator_name: "ArithmeticOperator",
  #                       start_offset: 8, end_offset: 17, replacement: "a - b * c"),
  #   Stryker::Mutant.new(id: 8, file: "lib/invoice.rb", mutator_name: "ArithmeticOperator",
  #                       start_offset: 12, end_offset: 17, replacement: "b / c"),
  # ]
  # result = Stryker::Instrumenter.instrument(source, mutants)
  # result.source
  # # => "total = (::Stryker::Active.id == 7 ? (a - b * c) : (::Stryker::Active.hit(7); (a + (::Stryker::Active.id == 8 ? (b / c) : (::Stryker::Active.hit(8); (b * c))))))\n"
  # result.skipped
  # # => []
  # ```
  #
  # A mutant inside `BEGIN { }` gets no switch, and the source comes back
  # unchanged:
  #
  # ```ruby
  # source = "BEGIN { puts 1 }\n"
  # mutant = Stryker::Mutant.new(id: 1, file: "lib/boot.rb", mutator_name: "NumberLiteral",
  #                              start_offset: 13, end_offset: 14, replacement: "2")
  # result = Stryker::Instrumenter.instrument(source, [mutant])
  # result.source == source
  # # => true
  # result.skipped.map(&:reason)
  # # => [:no_switch_site]
  # ```
  class Instrumenter
    # Instruments `source` with `mutants`, and returns the instrumented
    # source with the mutants that got no switch. Raises
    # `Stryker::Instrumenter::Error` when `source` doesn't parse, or when
    # every plain mutant parses and the instrumented source doesn't. That is
    # a fault in the instrumenter or in a mutator, as when a replacement ends
    # with a comment that hides less of the plain mutant than of the switch.
    #
    # Every mutant must be an edit on `source`. Mutants at the same site
    # share one switch, which tries them in the order of their IDs. With no
    # mutants, the result's source equals `source`. The instrumenter parses
    # the whole file with Prism once, once more for each mutant that has a
    # node to hold its switch, as the plain mutant, and once more for each
    # mutant whose text it puts on one line with refract. A mutant with no
    # such node gets no plain mutant, and no parse.
    #
    # The parses of the plain mutants are the cost of a large file. A file
    # whose parses add up to twice
    # `Stryker::Instrumenter::ParsePool::WORK_PER_WORKER` bytes or more has
    # them done in a pool of fresh `ruby` processes, `concurrency` at most
    # (`Stryker::Instrumenter::ParsePool`), and a smaller file has them done
    # in this process. The result is the same either way. Every process of the
    # pool ends before `instrument` returns, or raises. It raises
    # `Stryker::Instrumenter::Error` when a pool process fails, with the ID and file of
    # the mutant it was parsing.
    #
    # - `source`: String, the file's source.
    # - `mutants`: Array of Stryker::Mutant, the edits on `source`.
    # - `concurrency`: Integer, how many pool processes to run at most. With
    #   1, every parse is in this process. The number of processors by
    #   default.
    def self.instrument: (String source, Array[Mutant] mutants, ?concurrency: Integer) -> Result

    # Parses `source`, ready to instrument it. Raises
    # `Stryker::Instrumenter::Error` when `source` doesn't parse.
    #
    # - `source`: String, the file's source.
    # - `pool`: Stryker::Instrumenter::ParsePool, which parses the plain
    #   mutants. It parses them all in this process by default. A caller
    #   that instruments many files gives each one the same pool, which keeps
    #   its processes from one file to the next, and stops the pool itself.
    def initialize: (String source, ?ParsePool pool) -> void

    # Instruments the source with `mutants`, as `Stryker::Instrumenter.instrument`
    # does.
    #
    # - `mutants`: Array of Stryker::Mutant, the edits on the source.
    def instrument: (Array[Mutant] mutants) -> Result

    private

    @source: String
    @tree: Prism::ProgramNode
    @bodies: Array[[Heredoc::node, Span]]
    @outcomes: Outcomes

    def parsed: (String output) -> String
  end
end
